WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.947
Use these slides actively.

00:00:01.947 --> 00:00:08.592
Read the idea first, pause for a moment, and predict what you think the code will do before you run it.

00:00:08.592 --> 00:00:13.427
After you run an example, change one value and run it again.

00:00:13.427 --> 00:00:19.902
Small changes are one of the best ways to understand Python because you can immediately see which part of the output

00:00:19.902 --> 00:00:21.522
depends on which input.

00:00:22.196 --> 00:00:27.937
For example, if sales is one thousand dollars, run the calculation once.

00:00:27.937 --> 00:00:31.997
Then change sales to twelve hundred fifty and see what changes.

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:34.937
Ask yourself why the result moved.

00:00:34.937 --> 00:00:43.057
The editor looks like VS Code, but the Python ideas are the same whether you use VS Code, Jupyter Notebook,

00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:45.649
Google Colab, or another environment.
